@@ -0,0 +1,148 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: x509-parser
+
+Target: `x509-parser` 0.19.0-beta.1 (rusticata/x509-parser), an X.509 v3 /
+DER parser (nom + asn1-rs 0.8.0-beta.1). Tests added with `hegeltest` 0.28.2
+(dev-dependency; added with `--ignore-rust-version`, hegeltest MSRV 1.86 >
+crate rust-version 1.85 — call this out if upstreaming).
+
+Maintainer-consent check: no `HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md`, `.ai-ack`, or anti-AI
+clause found in the repo (no CONTRIBUTING file at all). Nothing was pushed or
+filed upstream; all work is local to this copy.
+
+## 1. What was tested and why
+
+X.509 certificates, CRLs, CSRs, and PEM containers are parsed from hostile
+input by design (TLS handshakes, CRL distribution points, enrollment
+endpoints, on-disk files). The crate's own docs promise panic-freedom and
+defensive parsing ("recursion limit, defensive programming ... aims to be
+panic-free"), and the repo carries four libfuzzer targets
+(`fuzz/fuzz_targets/{x509_parse,crl,certreq,x509_with_mutator}.rs`) — strong
+evidence the maintainers consider byte-level robustness the key property.
+The hegel tests supplement those fuzzers with structured, seeded, shrinkable
+variants and with oracle-independent structural invariants the fuzzers do not
+check. 9 properties, added to the existing integration test files (in
+`mod hegel_props` at the end of each):
+
+`tests/readcert.rs` (7 properties, seeded by the 10 valid DER certificates in
+`assets/`):
+1. `prop_cert_arbitrary_bytes_never_panic` — arbitrary bytes (raw and
+   `0x30 0x82`-prefixed) through `parse_x509_certificate`,
+   `X509Certificate::from_der`, and `X509Certificate::parse_der` never panic.
+2. `prop_cert_crafted_length_header_errs` — a long-form DER length header
+   (1–8 attacker-controlled length octets, up to 2^64-scale claimed lengths)
+   claiming more content than available must return `Err` — quickly and
+   without pre-allocating the claimed length (an allocation would OOM-abort
+   the test).
+3. `prop_cert_corrupted_seed_never_panics` — valid-then-corrupt: 0–8 byte
+   mutations (biased toward the leading tag/length bytes) of a real cert
+   parse to Ok or Err, never panic; on Ok, zero-copy slices stay in-bounds.
+4. `prop_cert_strict_prefix_errs` — truncation: every strict prefix of a
+   valid cert returns `Err` (the outer SEQUENCE length always covers the
+   whole cert), never panics.
+5. `prop_parsed_cert_slices_within_input` — parsed-field bounds
+   (oracle-independent): every zero-copy slice exposed by a parsed cert
+   (`as_raw`, TBS `as_ref`, `raw_serial`, issuer/subject `as_raw`, extension
+   values, remainder) lies within the input allocation, checked by pointer
+   range against a fresh copy of the seed.
+6. `prop_cert_reparse_equal` — re-parsing exactly the consumed bytes yields
+   an equal `X509Certificate` (derived `PartialEq`) with empty remainder.
+7. `prop_deeply_nested_der_no_stack_overflow` — SEQUENCE/SET nested 2–50,000
+   deep (deep end boosted; uniform draws rarely land there) is rejected
+   gracefully by the cert/CRL/CSR parsers and survives `X509Name::parse_der`
+   — no stack overflow, no hang.
+
+`tests/readcrl.rs` (seeded by the 8 CRL fixtures incl. `crl-ext/` and
+`crl-idp/`):
+8. `prop_crl_arbitrary_and_corrupted_never_panic` + `prop_crl_strict_prefix_errs`
+   — same arbitrary/corrupt/truncate scheme for `parse_x509_crl` /
+   `CertificateRevocationList::from_der`; on Ok, walks
+   `iter_revoked_certificates` and its accessors (`raw_serial_as_string`,
+   `reason_code`, `invalidity_date`, `crl_number`) without panicking.
+
+`tests/readcsr.rs` (seeded by the 4 CSR fixtures, PEM ones decoded first):
+9. `prop_csr_arbitrary_and_corrupted_never_panic` + `prop_csr_strict_prefix_errs`
+   — same scheme for `X509CertificationRequest::from_der`; on Ok, walks
+   attributes and `requested_extensions()`.
+
+`tests/pem.rs`:
+- `prop_pem_arbitrary_input_never_panics` — raw garbage and
+  BEGIN/END-wrapped arbitrary bodies (so the base64 decoder and inner DER
+  parse are reached) never panic; `Pem::iter_from_buffer` terminates (a
+  10,000-iteration cap converts a would-be infinite loop into a failure).
+- `prop_pem_corrupted_seed_never_panics` — mutated real PEM parses to Ok or
+  Err without panicking, including the inner `parse_x509` of corrupted
+  base64 payload.
+
+(Counted as 9 distinct properties: 1–7 above plus the CRL and CSR groups;
+the PEM pair rides along in `tests/pem.rs`.)
+
+## 2. Oracles used
+
+Deliberately oracle-independent (per the skill's guidance for a mature,
+heavily-fuzzed parser):
+
+- **No-panic / no-abort / no-hang** — the process itself is the oracle.
+- **Structural containment** — zero-copy output slices must lie inside the
+  input allocation (pointer-range check against a fresh `Vec` copy); an
+  over-read cannot pass this even accidentally.
+- **Grammar-forced rejection** — DER's definite-length framing guarantees a
+  strict prefix and an over-claiming length header cannot be a complete
+  object, so `is_err()` is asserted (not merely "no panic") for truncation
+  and crafted-length inputs.
+- **Self-agreement** — re-parse of consumed bytes equals the first parse
+  (derived `PartialEq`), input held by the test.
+- **Resource caps** — the crafted-length test doubles as an OOM probe (a
+  huge claimed length that triggered allocation would abort); the PEM
+  iterator has an explicit iteration cap.
+
+## 3. Bugs found: none
+
+No panics, stack overflows, OOMs, hangs, out-of-bounds slices, or wrongly
+accepted truncated/over-claiming inputs were found — at the default 100
+cases and at a 10x (1,000-case) exploratory run of every property.
+
+This matches expectations: the rusticata crates are continuously fuzzed
+(this repo ships four libfuzzer targets including an ASN.1-aware custom
+mutator adapted from NSS), and asn1-rs 0.8's DER mode reads definite-length
+content iteratively with an explicit `MAX_RECURSION = 50` for the BER paths,
+so the classic nested-DER stack overflow is designed out. The deep-nesting
+and crafted-length properties are still worth keeping: they pin the two
+security promises (no unbounded recursion, no attacker-controlled
+allocation) that a future refactor could silently break.
+
+## 4. How to run
+
+```bash
+cd targets/x509-parser-fable
+cargo test                       # full suite, hegel properties included
+cargo test -- hegel_props        # just the property tests
+```
+
+No `#[ignore]`d reproducers exist (nothing process-aborting was found).
+`.hegel/` (failure database) is gitignored.
+
+## 5. Confidence / validation notes
+
+- **Liveness**: inverting the assertion in `prop_cert_strict_prefix_errs`
+  produced an immediate hegel failure; reverted, `.hegel/` deleted after.
+- **Guarded branches**: the `if let Ok(...)` branches in the
+  valid-then-corrupt properties were counted with temporary `eprintln!`s.
+  Initially the CRL/CSR Ok-branches fired **0/100** times (vacuous — 1–8
+  mutations of small, mostly-structural files almost never survive), so the
+  mutation-count generator was changed to include 0 mutations (guaranteeing
+  the accepted-input branch every run) and to bias toward 1–2 mutations.
+  After the fix: cert 16, CRL 35, CSR 46, PEM 19 (+12 for the
+  BEGIN/END-wrapped body reaching a successful PEM parse) fires per
+  100-case run. Instrumentation removed afterwards.
+- **10x exploratory run**: all properties run at `test_cases = 1000` with a
+  per-case counter confirming ≥1000 cases actually executed (the two
+  fixed-seed-only properties, `prop_cert_reparse_equal` and
+  `prop_parsed_cert_slices_within_input`, exhausted their 10-seed domain at
+  10 cases — expected). All green; instrumentation then removed and counts
+  restored to the default.
+- Edition-2018 gotcha encountered: inline `{name}` format captures silently
+  don't interpolate (and `format!("-----BEGIN {label}-----")` would have
+  produced a *literal* `{label}` marker, quietly weakening the PEM
+  property); all format strings use explicit arguments.
+- Pre-existing suite (readcert/readcrl/readcsr/pem/test01/verify +
+  doctests) still fully green alongside the new properties.
